"""Form Three History Term One: rows 1–13 and 15–19; guided-work row 14 excluded.
Dates in displayed teaching text are expression literals, never bare digits.
"""
from codex_support import *
F=fact
def unit(n,title,note,case,method,facts,**kw):
    concept_lesson("history","y3-",n,title,note,case,method,facts,"colonial-history",**kw)

unit(1,"Cameroonian societies before annexation",
 "Before German annexation, the territory now called Cameroon contained diverse political communities, economies and belief systems. Some communities had central rulers and courts; others distributed authority among councils, lineage heads or associations. Farming, craft production and local and long-distance exchange supported livelihoods. Coastal intermediaries connected inland producers with overseas commerce. These societies changed over time and should not be described as one identical political system or as having no history before Europeans arrived.",
 "A class can compare a Grassfields kingdom's institutions with coastal Duala trading networks without claiming that either represented every community. A market exchange, a court decision and a lineage obligation concern different parts of social life. Today's national borders should not simply be projected backwards as the boundaries of a single precolonial state.",
 "Ask which community, period and source are being described. Compare authority, production and social relations separately. Treat an oral account or archaeological object as evidence to examine, not as inferior merely because it is not a European written record. Avoid generalising one case to all Cameroon.",[
 F("centralised authority","political power concentrated in a ruler and supporting institutions","A historical kingdom has a court and officials serving its ruler.","All precolonial communities lacked political institutions.","Courts, rulers and other institutions existed in several different forms."),
 F("distributed authority","political roles shared among several local institutions","Councils and lineage heads share decisions in a community.","A community without one central monarch must have no government.","Authority can be organised through several institutions rather than one monarch."),
 F("production","activities providing material goods and livelihoods","People cultivate crops and make tools before annexation.","Economic activity began only with colonial rule.","Farming and crafts were established activities before colonial annexation."),
 F("intermediary trade","exchange connecting different producer and buyer networks","Coastal traders link inland produce with overseas merchants.","A trading intermediary has no role between producer and overseas buyer.","Intermediaries connect and influence exchange networks.")])

unit(2,"European powers on the Cameroon coast before annexation",
 "Overseas contact developed before formal colonial rule. Portuguese arrivals were recorded on the coast in the fifteenth century; later British, German and other trading connections grew. Merchants sought products and access, while missions pursued religious and educational activities. African traders and rulers negotiated these relationships and protected their own interests. Coastal commerce was not the same thing as possession of the whole interior. The existence of a European ship or mission station does not establish that all local communities had surrendered sovereignty.",
 "Duala traders acted between inland producers and overseas firms. British commercial and missionary ties were established before the German claim in {1884}. German firms also built interests on the coast. Comparing their roles helps explain later rivalry, but European records must be read alongside evidence of African choices and authority.",
 "Distinguish contact, trade, mission work and formal political control. Ask what area a source actually describes. Do not use the colonial language of discovery to imply that land was unknown to its inhabitants. Identify the interests and standpoint of the person writing the record.",[
 F("commercial contact","overseas exchange before formal political annexation","A merchant exchanges goods with coastal trading intermediaries.","Every commercial exchange transfers sovereignty over the whole interior.","Trade and a transfer of political authority are different relationships."),
 F("mission activity","religious work that could include schooling and translation","A mission establishes a congregation and teaches reading.","Every mission worker was simply a colonial governor.","Mission and governing roles differed even when their activities intersected."),
 F("African agency","local actors pursuing their own aims in relationships","A coastal ruler negotiates conditions to protect trade and land.","African rulers made no choices during overseas contact.","Local actors negotiated, resisted and pursued interests, although power was unequal."),
 F("limited coastal reach","the distinction between coastal influence and control of the interior","A firm's station serves one coastal trading network.","One coastal station proves effective rule over every inland community.","The extent of actual authority needs evidence; it cannot be assumed from a station.")])

unit(3,"The European scramble for Cameroon",
 "The scramble for Cameroon involved competing commercial and imperial ambitions, local negotiations and unequal power. German firms encouraged official protection of their interests. Some coastal rulers had previously sought British protection, while British official responses did not secure control before the German action. In July {1884}, treaties and a German flag ceremony marked the coastal claim. This was a beginning of colonial expansion, not proof that every inland community had agreed or that all resistance had ended.",
 "The German-Duala treaty conventionally dated {12} July {1884} and Nachtigal's flag raising on {14} July must be distinguished. A document signed by particular rulers had a specific scope. The later extension of German control required further diplomacy, coercion and military action. Do not compress these different stages into one event covering the whole modern territory.",
 "Build a sequence: prior contact and rivalry, coastal negotiations, imperial claim and inland expansion. Ask whose authority a treaty could actually bind. Compare the wording of a claim with the extent of control on the ground and with evidence of dissent.",[
 F("commercial pressure","firms encouraging official action to protect their interests","German trading interests urge imperial involvement on the coast.","Commercial firms were completely unrelated to imperial lobbying.","Trading interests helped encourage official protection and colonial claims."),
 F("treaty negotiation","discussion and signing of terms by particular parties","Coastal rulers and German representatives agree a document with stated conditions.","A treaty involving some coastal rulers automatically binds every inland polity.","The parties' authority and the document's geographic scope must be examined."),
 F("imperial claim","a state's assertion of colonial authority","A German flag ceremony publicly asserts a protectorate claim.","A flag ceremony proves that all inhabitants freely consented.","An assertion of authority is not evidence of universal consent."),
 F("consolidation","extension and enforcement of an initially asserted authority","Officials later send expeditions to extend inland control.","No further action was required after the first coastal claim.","The spread of control was a separate and contested process.")])

unit(4,"Explorers, missions, traders and imperial agents in Africa",
 "Explorers produced travel records and maps using assistance and knowledge from African guides and communities. Missionaries promoted religion and often education or translation. Traders sought commercial returns; statesmen and officials negotiated or imposed imperial claims; soldiers enforced expansion. Their activities could overlap but should not be treated as one occupation. Knowledge collected by travellers could assist later imperial projects, while African responses differed. A claimed contribution should be examined alongside coercion, extraction and the interests of those affected.",
 "A travel map, a mission school record, a trading agreement and an official proclamation answer different historical questions. Stanley's activities in the Congo region can be related to Leopold's imperial project without confusing that region with Cameroon. African guides' contribution must not disappear simply because a European traveller authored the surviving book.",
 "Identify actor, action, place and purpose. Distinguish an actor's stated motive from an effect of the activity. Do not assume every school or map had identical consequences. A source may advertise an imperial achievement while omitting the local costs or knowledge on which it depended.",[
 F("exploration record","travel knowledge and mapping collected during an expedition","A traveller records routes learned with local guides.","A European map proves that local people knew nothing about their land.","Recorded maps often depended on existing African knowledge."),
 F("missionary activity","religious work often linked to education or translation","A mission translates a religious text and runs classes.","Schooling alone proves every mission opposed all imperial interests.","Activities and political relationships varied; one activity does not settle every motive."),
 F("commercial enterprise","business activity seeking returns from exchange or production","A company establishes a trading station for goods.","A company station is necessarily a charitable project with no commercial aims.","Commercial enterprises seek economic returns, even when they also claim other aims."),
 F("imperial enforcement","official or military action extending colonial authority","Armed agents impose a claim after negotiations fail.","Armed expansion is identical to an equal voluntary trade.","Military coercion and voluntary exchange are different relationships.")])

unit(5,"Motives for the European scramble for Africa",
 "European expansion had interacting economic, political and social or ideological motives. Firms sought markets, raw materials and profitable opportunities. Governments pursued prestige, strategic positions and advantage against rivals. Missionary ambitions and claims of a civilising mission supplied ideological arguments; racist assumptions were used to justify unequal rule. No single motive explains every region or actor. Distinguish the reasons actors gave publicly from the interests and actions supported by evidence.",
 "A merchant's request for protected trade, a government's concern over a rival flag and a mission appeal for conversion point to different motives. They could reinforce one another. Explaining an imperial ideology does not mean accepting its racist premises or treating colonisation as a benevolent necessity.",
 "Classify the evidence rather than guessing from nationality. Ask whether it concerns commercial gain, power and security, or beliefs and claimed duties. Consider a combination when a case supports several motives. Avoid the claim that every European or every African shared one identical purpose.",[
 F("economic motive","an aim concerning markets resources or returns","A firm seeks access to produce and customers.","A search for profitable sales is only a religious motive.","Markets and commercial returns concern economic aims."),
 F("political motive","an aim concerning prestige power or rivalry","A government fears a rival empire will claim a strategic coast.","Imperial rivalry is simply a household purchase.","Competition between states for influence is political."),
 F("strategic motive","an aim concerning access routes bases or security","An empire seeks a harbour useful for ships.","A useful naval harbour matters only as a decorative landscape.","Access and naval position can give a harbour strategic importance."),
 F("ideological justification","a belief or claim used to legitimise expansion","An imperial advocate claims an unequal civilising duty.","Explaining a racist justification means proving that it is morally or factually right.","A historian can examine an ideology critically without endorsing it.")])

unit(6,"How Germany established its Cameroon claim",
 "German firms helped prepare coastal agreements and encouraged imperial action. Gustav Nachtigal acted as the imperial commissioner. The German-Duala treaty conventionally dated {12} July {1884} preceded the flag ceremony on {14} July. British Consul Edward Hewett arrived after the German claim. Timing, commercial preparation and diplomatic action mattered, but a coastal assertion was not immediate control of all Cameroon. Later expansion involved further agreements and violence, and local opposition continued.",
 "Compare the functions of a trader, a coastal signatory and an imperial commissioner. They are not interchangeable. A timeline should distinguish the treaty date from the flag date and then leave room for the later spread of rule. The Berlin Conference began later that year and did not itself sign the coastal treaty.",
 "Use dates to test causal statements: an event occurring later cannot be the earlier event's signing. Ask what a document claims and whose authority it represents. The formula Germany succeeded should describe the establishment of an imperial claim, not imply that colonial coercion was morally justified.",[
 F("German trading firms","commercial interests that helped prepare coastal agreements","Representatives of Woermann and Jantzen and Thormählen participate in arrangements.","The German flag was raised by an independent local farmers' association.","Commercial representatives and imperial officials had identifiable roles in the claim."),
 F("Gustav Nachtigal","the German imperial commissioner associated with the flag ceremony","An imperial commissioner raises the German flag in July {1884}.","Nachtigal was the British consul who arrived after the German claim.","Nachtigal represented Germany; Hewett was the British consul."),
 F("coastal treaty","an agreement with particular rulers before the flag ceremony","An agreement is dated {12} July {1884} in the school chronology.","The coastal treaty was signed at the later Berlin Conference.","The coastal treaty and the Berlin meeting were different events."),
 F("Edward Hewett","the British consul arriving after the German assertion","A British official reaches the coast after the German flag ceremony.","Hewett was the German commissioner who raised the flag.","The British consul and German imperial commissioner should not be confused.")])

unit(7,"Coastal chiefs' differing attitudes",
 "Coastal rulers did not hold one uniform view of European claims. Some negotiated with a power in the hope of protecting trade, land or political interests; some had sought British protection; others opposed the German claim. Bell and Akwa participated in agreements, but conditions and interpretations mattered. Lock Priso of Hickory Town resisted the German assertion. Cooperation by a ruler should not be treated as consent by every subject or as approval of every later colonial policy.",
 "A document expressing a wish to retain land or an intermediary trading position shows bargaining, not an empty formality. Later violations of expectations could produce protest. Compare a ruler's decision, the views of other local groups and the power of the colonial representative. Do not force every response into a permanent collaborator-or-resister identity.",
 "Ask what was sought, what was promised and what later occurred. Name the actor and period. A signature is evidence of a particular transaction, not proof that terms were understood identically or that the weaker side had equal bargaining power.",[
 F("negotiated cooperation","working with a power in pursuit of specific local aims","A coastal ruler seeks safeguards for land and trade in an agreement.","Negotiation means the ruler had no interests of his own.","Negotiators could pursue land, trade and political aims within unequal conditions."),
 F("opposition","refusal or resistance to an asserted claim","Lock Priso rejects German authority in Hickory Town.","Every coastal ruler willingly accepted German authority.","Responses differed; Lock Priso provides a counterexample to universal acceptance."),
 F("conditional expectation","a safeguard or limit a party expects an agreement to respect","A ruler expects land rights to remain protected.","A stated safeguard has no bearing on later disagreement.","An alleged breach of expected safeguards can explain later protest."),
 F("internal disagreement","different attitudes within a community","A ruler's decision is challenged by some people under his authority.","A ruler's signature proves that every subject agreed.","Communities contain differences; a leader's act cannot establish unanimous consent.")])

unit(8,"The Berlin West Africa Conference",
 "The Berlin Conference met from November {1884} to February {1885}, hosted by Bismarck. Participating powers addressed trade and navigation and rules for colonial claims, including notification and effective occupation in the relevant provisions. African rulers did not participate as equal decision makers. The conference helped regulate imperial rivalry; it did not draw every present African boundary at one sitting. Germany's initial Cameroon claim preceded the meeting. Distinguish the General Act from later treaties, conquests and border negotiations.",
 "The German Foreign Office archive describes the conference's framework rather than a complete map already divided in a room. A learner should compare that distinction with the common school shorthand partition of Africa. The shorthand captures imperial exclusion and competition, but should not erase the later processes by which specific boundaries and colonial control developed.",
 "Separate participants, aims, provisions and effects. Ask whether a proposed answer describes the meeting itself or later colonial expansion. Do not claim that a European agreement established African popular consent. Date the Cameroon coastal claim and the conference in the correct order.",[
 F("Bismarck's hosting role","the German chancellor's organisation of the Berlin meeting","Representatives meet in Berlin under German diplomatic hosting.","An African assembly elected Bismarck to represent all African societies.","Bismarck represented imperial Germany, not an elected African mandate."),
 F("trade and navigation provisions","rules concerning commerce and specified African waterways","Delegates discuss navigation on the Congo and Niger.","The meeting dealt only with European school timetables.","Trade and river navigation were explicit conference concerns."),
 F("rules for colonial claims","conditions aimed at regulating claims among imperial powers","Powers discuss notification and effective occupation.","A claim announced on paper always required no further action.","The relevant occupation provisions were meant to regulate competing claims."),
 F("African exclusion","absence of African peoples as equal conference decision makers","Imperial powers make rules about Africa without equal African representation.","The conference was a referendum of African communities.","An imperial diplomatic conference was not an African popular vote.")])

unit(9,"Expansion and consolidation of German rule",
 "After the coastal claim, Germany extended control inland through expeditions, treaties, military campaigns, stations and alliances. Consolidation involved making authority effective, collecting revenue and securing labour and routes. Existing African states and communities were not empty spaces waiting to be administered. Expansion was uneven and contested, and local intermediaries influenced its course. A map of a claimed colony should not be mistaken for proof that every locality was immediately under equally effective control.",
 "An official station could support communication and administration, while soldiers and armed expeditions imposed demands. A ruler might negotiate in one period and oppose a later demand. Study the relationship between claimed territory, actual institutions and evidence of resistance rather than assuming that a flag instantaneously completed conquest.",
 "Identify the method and the place. Compare a colonial claim with evidence of stations, enforcement and local response. Describe coercion explicitly when force or threat shaped an agreement. Treat alliances as historical choices in changing conditions, not proof that one people had a fixed unchanging attitude.",[
 F("military expedition","armed movement intended to impose or extend authority","Soldiers enter a resisting locality to enforce a colonial demand.","An armed punitive expedition is a neutral population survey.","Its coercive purpose must be distinguished from observation alone."),
 F("administrative station","a local base supporting colonial authority","Officials establish a post for communication and enforcement.","A station proves identical control over every neighbouring community.","A local base is evidence of a presence, not universal effective control."),
 F("alliance","cooperation between actors for particular aims","A local ruler cooperates with colonial agents against a rival.","An alliance proves permanent agreement on every later policy.","Alliances can be limited and change with circumstances."),
 F("contested expansion","extension of authority challenged by local action","A claimed area contains communities that oppose imposed demands.","A coloured colonial map proves all local opposition has disappeared.","A claim on a map and control accepted on the ground are not the same.")])

unit(10,"Responses to German rule: resistance and protest",
 "Resistance to German expansion included armed defence, petitions and refusal of imposed demands. Bakweri, Bangwa, Mamfe/Cross River and Nso campaigns had distinct causes and sequences. Early coastal conflict must also be separated from the later Duala land protest associated with Rudolf Duala Manga Bell. Compare demand, response and consequence, keeping African decisions visible. An official label such as rebellion does not prove that a community had freely accepted colonial authority. A military defeat did not erase continuing opposition or the costs of colonial rule.",
 "Compare a labour-recruitment dispute in Bangwa with German intrusion into the Cross River region. Both involved force, but they were not one campaign. A Duala petition used a different method. Source comparison should preserve these differences rather than substituting a list of heroic names for an explanation.",
 "For each case, connect the pressure to the response and outcome. Distinguish an initial victory from a later defeat and an armed campaign from a legal appeal. Compare colonial reports with local accounts and scholarship. Where accounts differ, identify the uncertainty rather than inventing an exact casualty total or quotation.",[
 F("Bakweri resistance","Kuva Likenye’s defence around Buea against German intrusion","Kuva Likenye’s forces repel the attack of {1891} before a renewed attack in {1894}.","The Bakweri victory in {1891} prevented every later German attack.","The initial success was followed by renewed attack and defeat in {1894}."),
 F("Bangwa resistance","opposition associated with Asunganyi and demands for plantation workers","A dispute over recruited workers precedes Bangwa resistance and German raids.","An initial agreement over workers proves Asunganyi accepted every later demand.","The relationship changed when disputes over recruitment developed."),
 F("Mamfe/Cross River resistance","armed opposition to German intrusion in the Upper Cross River region","The fighting of {1904} includes attacks on an expedition and trading posts, followed by German reprisals.","The Mamfe conflict was simply the Duala land petition in the same place.","The Cross River armed conflict and the coastal land petition were separate cases."),
 F("Nso resistance","defence of Nso authority through conflicts culminating in imposed control","Nso encounters include the fighting of {1902} and the campaign of {1906}.","Pavel’s presence and every later Nso campaign were one event with no chronological difference.","Separate the encounters and campaigns; the circumstances of Ngonnso’s removal also require careful sourcing."),
 F("Duala land protest","appeals against colonial land expropriation associated with Rudolf Duala Manga Bell","Duala leaders contest land removal through petitions and legal appeals.","Only a battlefield fight can count as resistance.","Formal appeals and land protest were also methods of resistance.")],
 sections_override=[
 ("Coastal and Bakweri responses","Lock Priso’s opposition in {1884} differed from later Duala land petitions. Rudolf Duala Manga Bell challenged expropriation and was executed in {1914}. Around Buea, Kuva Likenye defended Bakweri authority and land against intrusion. His forces repelled the German attack in {1891}; a better-armed attack in {1894} defeated them. An early victory did not ensure permanent independence."),
 ("Bangwa: recruitment and changing relations","Asunganyi initially received Gustav Conrau, who recruited workers for coastal plantations in {1898}. When Conrau sought more workers without the earlier recruits returning, relations deteriorated. Conrau was detained and died after attempting escape; accounts differ about the precise circumstances. Bangwa resistance met repeated German raids, destruction and seizures. Asunganyi continued opposition before his capture and exile in {1911}. Initial cooperation did not mean unconditional consent."),
 ("Mamfe and the Upper Cross River","Colonial intrusion, coercion and commercial demands provoked opposition among communities around the Cross River. In {1904}, Pückler-Limpurg’s expedition met armed resistance; he died during the fighting. Attacks also targeted trading posts. German forces retaliated through destructive campaigns, including burning settlements and crops and executing opponents. Colonial control followed severe repression. Mamfe is a regional label here, not proof that every community had one ruler or identical aims."),
 ("Nso: distinguish the encounters","Nso defended its authority amid unequal colonial demands. Pavel’s armed presence must be distinguished from Houben’s attack in {1902} and the further campaign in {1906}, after which German control and tribute demands were imposed. The palace suffered burning and looting during military violence. Ngonnso’s removal has a contested acquisition history: do not assign it to a particular battle without evidence. Compare local memory and colonial records, then explain each campaign’s causes, methods and consequences.")])

unit(11,"Political, economic and social change in German Kamerun",
 "German colonial rule created administrative institutions and expanded export-oriented production and transport. Plantations, roads and railways were connected to colonial economic aims; land and labour demands imposed serious costs. Mission and government activity brought forms of schooling and health provision but access was unequal and cultural change was contested. Calling a project development does not answer who controlled it, who benefited or who paid the costs. Assess different dimensions and communities rather than giving one unqualified verdict.",
 "A railway can carry produce and people while also serving extraction and colonial control. A plantation can increase export output while taking land and demanding labour. A school record can show educational activity without proving universal access. The useful historical question is not merely whether a facility existed, but how it operated in an unequal colonial system.",
 "For each change, identify its purpose, beneficiaries and burdens. Separate infrastructure, political control and social institutions. Avoid using one building as proof that colonial rule was universally beneficial or using the existence of coercion to deny that any material change occurred.",[
 F("colonial administration","institutions enforcing the imperial governing framework","Officials and stations organise revenue collection and authority.","A colonial institution was elected equally by every affected community.","Imperial authority did not rest on universal local democratic consent."),
 F("export orientation","production organised substantially for overseas markets","Plantation produce is transported toward the coast for export.","Export production necessarily serves only local subsistence needs.","Export orientation directs output to external markets rather than only household consumption."),
 F("coercive labour demand","work required through force or unequal colonial compulsion","Workers face imposed labour obligations linked to colonial projects.","All colonial labour demands were freely chosen equal contracts.","Force, penalties and unequal authority must be considered when assessing labour."),
 F("unequal access","benefits or services distributed unevenly","A school serves some localities while others have limited access.","The existence of one school proves universal schooling.","An institution's existence does not establish equal access for everyone.")])

unit(12,"Effects and weaknesses of the Berlin settlement",
 "The conference strengthened a framework for imperial competition while excluding African political equality. It assisted claims and expansion but did not itself implement every conquest or draw all later borders. Its proclaimed humanitarian and commercial language must be compared with subsequent coercion and exploitation. Boundaries and colonial policies could disrupt existing relations or divide connected communities. Results varied by place. The process should be assessed through its rules, implementation and affected peoples, not through the conference's stated aims alone.",
 "A rule intended to reduce conflict among imperial states did not guarantee peace for African communities resisting conquest. A trade provision did not guarantee equal bargaining power. When comparing a conference document with later events, identify what the document promises and what additional evidence is needed to judge the outcome.",
 "Separate aims from effects and framework from implementation. Avoid saying every modern border was drawn at Berlin. A criticism is stronger when it identifies a specific exclusion or consequence and links it to evidence rather than repeating a slogan alone.",[
 F("imperial framework","rules coordinating claims among competing colonial powers","States use conference rules when presenting colonial claims.","A framework means every conquest happened during the meeting itself.","Rules and later implementation are distinct stages."),
 F("exclusion of consent","decisions made without the affected peoples as equal participants","African political communities do not choose the conference's rules through a vote.","Imperial delegates automatically represented every African person's wishes.","Affected peoples were not equal decision makers in the conference."),
 F("implementation gap","a difference between proclaimed aims and actual practice","A humanitarian claim is compared with evidence of coercion.","A benevolent phrase proves that all later practice was benevolent.","Stated aims must be tested against actions and consequences."),
 F("disruption of existing relations","changes that divide or reshape established communities and networks","Later boundaries affect connected trading communities.","Every boundary necessarily followed all existing local relationships perfectly.","Colonial boundary making could cut across existing connections.")],prerequisites=[8])

unit(13,"Methods of acquiring colonial territory",
 "Imperial acquisition used treaties, protectorate claims, annexation, concessions and military conquest, often in combination. A concession granted specified rights, not automatically full sovereignty over a whole region. A protectorate claim asserted outside control under a language of protection; annexation claimed incorporation. Military conquest imposed control through force. Mandates belonged to the post-First World War framework and should not be placed before the Cameroon claim in {1884}. The historical label does not settle the legitimacy or fairness of the process.",
 "A company might obtain a concession and then seek official backing. A treaty could be presented as agreement while being shaped by threat or unequal interpretation. After the First World War, former German territories were dealt with under a different international framework. Keep these legal-political forms distinct from the practical degree of control actually achieved.",
 "Ask which rights were claimed, by whom and at what date. Distinguish a resource concession from a transfer of government powers. Do not turn the existence of a written document into evidence that coercion was absent. Sequence mandates after the war rather than using the term for every early annexation.",[
 F("concession","a grant of specified rights such as commercial exploitation","A company is allowed particular trading or resource rights.","Any concession automatically grants sovereignty over every neighbouring community.","The specified rights and geographic scope must be read rather than assumed."),
 F("protectorate claim","an asserted relationship of external protection and control","An imperial state announces protection while claiming governing influence.","Protection terminology proves equality and freedom from domination.","The label could accompany unequal external control."),
 F("military conquest","imposition of control through armed force","Troops defeat resistance and impose demands.","Conquest is identical to an agreement freely accepted without threat.","Armed coercion is different from an equal voluntary arrangement."),
 F("mandate","a post-First World War international arrangement over former territories","A former German territory is allocated for administration after the war.","The League mandate system created Germany's initial Cameroon claim in {1884}.","The mandate system belongs to a later postwar framework.")])

unit(15,"Reasons for African cooperation with Europeans",
 "African cooperation with European actors could be motivated by trade, security, political rivalry, diplomacy, access to education or an attempt to preserve authority under pressure. It did not necessarily mean endorsement of permanent colonial domination. Local actors made choices in unequal and changing circumstances. Some cooperated in one matter while resisting another. Explain a specific actor's situation rather than assigning one motive to all Africans or judging a choice without examining the available alternatives.",
 "A ruler facing a local rival might seek an outside alliance; a trader might seek access to a commercial network. A community may negotiate to reduce immediate harm rather than welcome every imposed demand. These original analytical examples illustrate possible motives, not invented quotations attributed to historical chiefs.",
 "Identify the desired benefit, the pressure and the risks. Ask whether cooperation concerned one project or the entire political relationship. Separate an explanation of a decision from moral approval of its consequences. Use local evidence before assigning a named leader a particular motive.",[
 F("commercial interest","an aim of protecting or extending exchange","A trader seeks access to an overseas buying network.","A trade agreement can never reflect a trader's own economic interests.","Commercial interests could motivate African as well as European actors."),
 F("security alliance","cooperation intended to reduce a threat or counter a rival","An imagined ruler seeks an ally against a neighbouring opponent.","An alliance proves unconditional support for every future colonial demand.","A security alliance may pursue a limited aim rather than unconditional approval."),
 F("negotiated accommodation","cooperation intended to preserve some room for local action","A community negotiates under pressure to protect particular interests.","A constrained choice proves the absence of pressure.","Accommodation can occur within unequal and coercive conditions."),
 F("changing response","an attitude that varies with conditions and demands","An actor cooperates over trade but later protests land removal.","Cooperation once prevents any later resistance.","Responses can change when policies and interests change.")])

unit(16,"General causes of African resistance",
 "Resistance could arise from defence of sovereignty, land, livelihoods, religion and social institutions. Taxes, labour demands and interference in established authority could provoke opposition. Different communities faced different pressures, so there was no single universal cause. Resistance also took different forms, from armed action to petitions, refusal and evasion. Explaining causes requires linking a demand or change to the interests it threatened and to the response that followed.",
 "A demand for land may threaten farming and political authority at once. An imposed labour requirement may remove workers from household production. A religious or cultural interference may be experienced as an attack on community institutions. These categories help organise evidence but should not replace the detailed story of a named resistance movement.",
 "Distinguish long-term grievances from an immediate trigger. Avoid calling resistance irrational merely because an official record labels it rebellion. Ask whose authority and livelihood were affected, and compare the accounts of those imposing and those opposing a demand.",[
 F("defence of sovereignty","opposition to losing political self-rule","A community rejects an outside authority's governing claim.","Defending local political authority is merely a dispute about weather.","The relevant issue is control over governing decisions."),
 F("land grievance","opposition to seizure or restriction of land","Farmers resist removal from fields they rely on.","Land loss cannot affect livelihoods.","Land could sustain production, residence and authority, so its loss mattered."),
 F("labour grievance","opposition to imposed work demands","Households object to workers being compelled away from their farms.","Compulsory labour is automatically a voluntary choice.","Compulsion and its household costs can explain opposition."),
 F("cultural or religious grievance","opposition to interference in valued beliefs or institutions","A community defends an institution targeted by an outside demand.","Cultural institutions have no possible relation to political resistance.","Beliefs and institutions could be connected to authority and collective life.")])

unit(17,"Abyssinian resistance: Adwa",
 "Ethiopian forces defeated an invading Italian army at Adwa in {1896}, under Menelik II's leadership with important roles for Ethiopian commanders and Empress Taytu. Disputes over Italian claims and the Wuchale treaty formed part of the background. Mobilisation, arms, leadership and local knowledge contributed to the outcome. The victory preserved independence against that invasion and became a wider symbol of resistance. It does not mean Ethiopia was never later invaded: the Italian assault in the twentieth century was a separate episode.",
 "A historical explanation should connect more than one factor rather than crediting victory to a single heroic name. A painting of Adwa can show later memory and symbolism, but its arrangement of figures is not necessarily a precise battlefield plan. The Smithsonian's object description explicitly distinguishes historical significance from artistic arrangement.",
 "Arrange background, mobilisation, battle and consequences. Distinguish military outcome from a claim of timeless immunity from invasion. When using art as evidence, ask when it was made and what it commemorates; do not read every detail as a contemporary eyewitness record.",[
 F("Menelik II's leadership","imperial Ethiopian leadership associated with mobilisation against Italy","Ethiopian forces unite against the invading army in the Adwa campaign.","Menelik II commanded the Italian invasion against Ethiopia.","Menelik led the Ethiopian side, not the invading Italian army."),
 F("treaty dispute","conflicting claims about the relationship described by Wuchale","Italy claims a protectorate relationship that Ethiopia rejects.","The disagreement concerned only the colour of battlefield uniforms.","The disputed political relationship was central, not a decorative detail."),
 F("Adwa victory","the Ethiopian defeat of the invading Italian army in {1896}","The invasion is defeated in a major battle and Ethiopian independence is upheld.","Adwa established permanent Italian colonial control immediately after the battle.","The battle defeated that attempt to impose colonial rule."),
 F("historical consequence","an outcome that must be placed in its specific period","Adwa becomes a symbol of African resistance beyond Ethiopia.","A victory in {1896} proves Ethiopia could never face any later invasion.","A particular victory does not rule out later events in another period.")])

unit(18,"Features of European colonial policies",
 "European colonial policies between {1900} and {1945} shared unequal imperial authority but differed across powers, regions and periods. Administration could rely more directly on officials or work through subordinated local rulers. Economic policies often sought revenue, labour and export production. Assimilation claimed incorporation into the coloniser's institutions and culture; association emphasised governing through distinctions and selected local arrangements. Neither label should be taken as a complete description of every colonial practice or as proof of equal rights.",
 "A French policy label, a British indirect-rule description and a plantation labour record answer different questions. Compare the intended model with how authority was exercised. A colonial official's promise of respect for custom may coexist with the power to depose a ruler or redirect local institutions.",
 "Compare political structure, economic demands and social policy separately. Note differences and common imperial inequality. Avoid treating all policies as identical, or using a national label as evidence that every locality followed one unchanging system.",[
 F("direct administration","rule relying substantially on colonial officials exercising authority","Officials directly supervise local decisions in a colonial district.","Direct administration means local people exercise equal sovereign control.","Colonial officials retain governing authority under the direct model."),
 F("indirect administration","rule using subordinated local institutions under colonial supervision","A recognised ruler carries out demands under a colonial resident's authority.","Indirect rule means full independence from colonial control.","Local office remains subordinated to imperial authority."),
 F("assimilation policy","an imperial claim of incorporating selected subjects into the coloniser's institutions and culture","A policy promotes the coloniser's language and institutional model.","An assimilation label proves universal equal citizenship in practice.","The policy claim and actual limited, unequal application must be distinguished."),
 F("association policy","an approach claiming to govern through distinctions and selected local arrangements","An administration invokes local institutions while retaining imperial control.","Association necessarily removes imperial hierarchy.","Using local arrangements does not abolish colonial subordination.")])

unit(19,"British indirect rule in Northern Nigeria",
 "Indirect rule used selected local rulers and institutions as part of colonial administration under British supervision. Lugard is closely associated with its Northern Nigerian model. Existing emirate structures, limited European personnel, cost concerns and a wish to govern through established authority contributed to its adoption. Rulers did not remain fully sovereign: colonial officials could direct policy and alter or remove office holders. The policy changed local institutions as well as using them, and outcomes varied when applied elsewhere.",
 "A resident could supervise an emir whose courts, officials and revenue institutions became part of the colonial framework. The appearance of local continuity did not make the arrangement independent of Britain. A later attempt to use the model in a different society might produce different difficulties; Northern Nigeria should not be assumed identical to every African region.",
 "Explain both the local machinery and the superior colonial authority. Distinguish an official justification from evidence of how the system worked. Assess cost and administrative convenience alongside coercion and changes to local power. Indirect is a method of colonial rule, not the absence of colonial rule.",[
 F("local intermediary authority","rulers and institutions used to implement colonial administration","A recognised emir and officials carry out administrative functions.","Using an emir means Britain surrendered all policy control.","The local authority operated within a colonial supervisory framework."),
 F("colonial supervision","imperial authority over the local administrative machinery","A resident directs policy and can challenge a ruler's conduct.","A colonial resident has no relation to how indirect rule operates.","Supervision is central to the subordinate status of the local machinery."),
 F("administrative economy","reliance on existing machinery to limit staffing and costs","A government with few European officials works through established offices.","Indirect rule required every village to have a large new European administration.","Limited staffing and costs helped encourage use of existing local institutions."),
 F("institutional change","alteration of local authority within the colonial system","A ruler's powers are redefined to meet colonial administrative aims.","Indirect rule preserved every custom and power exactly unchanged.","Colonial supervision and new functions could transform local institutions.")])

